    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">Workers comp adjuster salary typically falls between $55,000 and $82,000 a year for staff adjusters, with senior and litigation specialists earning more. It is one of the steadiest paths in claims: mostly desk-based, mostly W-2, and driven by workplace injuries that happen every day rather than by storm seasons.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Most workers compensation claims adjusters earn $55,000 to $82,000 a year. Entry-level and medical-only adjusters often start in the mid-$50,000s, lost-time adjusters with a few years of experience commonly earn $65,000 to $82,000, and senior or litigation adjusters can reach $85,000+. Most roles are salaried with full benefits.</div>

<p>Workers compensation adjusters manage claims for employees hurt on the job. They confirm coverage and compensability, authorize medical treatment, calculate and pay wage replacement benefits, coordinate return to work, and resolve claims, sometimes through settlement with attorneys involved.</p>

<h2 id="workers-comp-adjuster-salary-ranges">Workers Comp Adjuster Salary Ranges</h2>

<p>These ranges reflect industry estimates and the job postings we track for staff roles. Your pay depends on employer, state, caseload type and settlement authority.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Typical base salary</th><th>Typical caseload</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Trainee / claims assistant</td><td>$45,000 - $55,000</td><td>Supports adjusters, sets up new claims</td></tr>
<tr><td>Medical-only adjuster</td><td>$52,000 - $62,000</td><td>High volume, simple treatment-only files</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lost-time adjuster</td><td>$60,000 - $82,000</td><td>Fewer, complex files with wage benefits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Senior / litigation adjuster</td><td>$75,000 - $95,000+</td><td>Attorney-represented, catastrophic and long-tail claims</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claims supervisor</td><td>$85,000 - $110,000+</td><td>Manages a team and reviews high-value reserves</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<p>The BLS reports a median of $76,790 a year for the broader group of claims adjusters, appraisers, examiners and investigators (May 2024). Experienced lost-time adjusters typically sit near that median. See our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/claims-adjuster-salary/">claims adjuster salary</a> guide for how this compares with other lines.</p>

<h2 id="what-workers-comp-adjusters-do">What a Workers Comp Adjuster Does Day to Day</h2>

<p>The pay reflects a job that mixes investigation, math, medicine and customer service. A typical week includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Taking recorded statements from injured workers, supervisors and witnesses</li>
<li>Deciding compensability within state deadlines and issuing acceptance or denial notices</li>
<li>Calculating average weekly wage and paying temporary disability benefits on time</li>
<li>Reviewing medical reports, approving treatment and working with nurse case managers</li>
<li>Coordinating light duty and return to work with the employer</li>
<li>Setting and updating reserves so the carrier or employer knows the expected cost</li>
<li>Negotiating settlements and preparing files for hearings or mediation</li>
</ul>
<p>Deadlines are strict. Late benefit payments can trigger penalties in many states, so organization matters as much as judgment. Adjusters who stay on top of diary dates and communicate clearly with injured workers keep complaints low, which is one of the metrics carriers and TPAs use when deciding raises.</p>

<h2 id="carrier-vs-tpa-vs-self-insured">Carrier vs TPA vs Self-Insured Employer</h2>

<p>Who you work for shapes your pay, caseload and day-to-day pressure.</p>

<h3>Insurance carriers</h3>
<p>Carriers that write workers compensation policies hire adjusters to handle claims for their policyholders. They tend to offer structured training, clear promotion ladders and solid benefits. Pay is usually mid-range to strong, and caseloads are managed by claim type.</p>

<h3>Third party administrators (TPAs)</h3>
<p>TPAs handle claims on behalf of carriers, self-insured employers and public entities. They are the largest employers of workers comp claims adjusters and hire a lot of entry-level talent. The tradeoff is caseload: TPA adjusters often carry more files, and pay can start slightly lower. The experience is excellent, and many adjusters use a TPA as a launch pad.</p>

<h3>Self-insured employers</h3>
<p>Large employers such as hospital systems, school districts, cities, retailers and manufacturers may self-insure and keep adjusters or claims managers in-house. These roles often pay well, carry lighter caseloads and come with public-sector or corporate benefits, but openings are scarce and usually go to experienced adjusters.</p>

<h2 id="lost-time-vs-medical-only-caseloads">Lost-Time vs Medical-Only Caseloads</h2>

<p>The biggest pay driver inside workers comp is the kind of claim you handle.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Medical-only claims</strong> involve treatment with little or no time off work. They close quickly and come in high volume. Adjusters may carry a large number of open files and focus on prompt bill review and payment.</li>
<li><strong>Lost-time claims</strong> involve wage replacement, return-to-work coordination, nurse case managers, independent medical exams and often attorneys. Caseloads are smaller but each file demands judgment, reserving skill and negotiation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Moving from medical-only to lost-time is the most common first promotion and usually comes with a meaningful raise. Litigated and catastrophic injury files, such as spinal cord or severe burn claims, sit at the top of the range.</p>

<div class="callout callout-tip"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Learn to calculate average weekly wage and benefit rates by hand for your state. Adjusters who get indemnity math right the first time avoid penalties and complaints, and that accuracy is exactly what supervisors look for when promoting to lost-time.</div>

<h2 id="workers-comp-adjuster-salary-texas">Workers Comp Adjuster Salary in Texas</h2>

<p>Texas is a large and unusual workers compensation market. It is the only state where private employers can opt out of workers' comp entirely. These employers are called nonsubscribers, and their injury claims are handled through occupational injury benefit plans or lawsuits rather than the state system. The rest of the system is overseen by the Texas Department of Insurance, Division of Workers' Compensation (DWC).</p>

<p>For adjusters, that means Texas work comes in two flavors: claims inside the DWC system, with its own benefit rules, deadlines and dispute process, and nonsubscriber plan claims. Adjusters handling Texas workers comp claims are generally expected to hold the right license and demonstrate Texas workers comp knowledge or certification.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Texas role (estimate)</th><th>Typical base salary</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Medical-only adjuster</td><td>$52,000 - $62,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lost-time adjuster</td><td>$60,000 - $82,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Senior / litigation adjuster</td><td>$75,000 - $95,000+</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<p>Texas pay tracks national ranges, with our statewide staff adjuster estimate near $76,800. Austin, Dallas-Fort Worth and Houston, where most carriers and TPAs have offices, tend to pay a bit more than San Antonio, El Paso or the Rio Grande Valley. See metro estimates in our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/insurance-adjuster-salary-texas/">Texas insurance adjuster salary</a> guide.</p>

<h2 id="state-jurisdiction-certifications">State Jurisdiction Knowledge and Certifications</h2>

<p>Workers compensation is governed state by state. Benefit formulas, waiting periods, medical fee schedules, filing deadlines and dispute processes all differ. That is why employers pay a premium for adjusters who know a specific jurisdiction well.</p>

<p>Several large states expect workers comp adjusters to demonstrate state-specific knowledge:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Texas:</strong> Has its own workers compensation system run by the TDI Division of Workers' Compensation and expects adjusters handling Texas claims to be licensed and familiar with Texas rules. Many adjusters start with the <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/texas-adjuster-license-salary/">Texas adjuster license</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Florida:</strong> Uses specific adjuster license classes for workers compensation and has its own benefit structure.</li>
<li><strong>California:</strong> Requires claims adjusters handling California workers comp to complete state-approved training and certification, and it is known as one of the most complex systems in the country.</li>
</ul>
<p>Requirements change, so confirm current rules with each state's regulator. Multi-state experience, especially California or another complex jurisdiction, is one of the clearest paths to the top of the workers compensation claims adjuster salary range. Our <a href="/salary-by-state/">insurance adjuster salary by state</a> page shows how regional pay differs, including <a href="/salary-by-state/#TX">Texas</a>.</p>

<h2 id="workers-comp-adjuster-salary-w2-stability">Workers Comp Adjuster Salary: Steady W-2 Desk Pay</h2>

<p>Unlike property and CAT adjusting, workers comp is almost entirely a desk job. Inspections are rare; field case managers or investigators handle on-site work when needed. That brings real advantages:</p>
<ul>
<li>Predictable weekday hours with little travel</li>
<li>Many remote and hybrid positions</li>
<li>Salaried W-2 pay with health insurance, retirement and paid time off</li>
<li>Income that does not depend on hurricane or hail seasons</li>
</ul>
<p>The tradeoff is a lower ceiling than a big storm year for an independent CAT adjuster, and a job that is emotionally demanding at times: injured workers are often worried about money and their health. If you want to compare, see our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/cat-adjuster-salary/">cat adjuster salary</a> guide.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-raise-workers-comp-adjuster-pay">How to Raise Your Workers Comp Adjuster Pay</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Get licensed early.</strong> An adjuster license makes you hireable at carriers and TPAs. A pre-licensing course from <a href="https://www.adjustingonline.com/courses/" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">AdjustingOnline</a> covers the exam. Use <strong>joe20</strong> for 20% off.</li>
<li><strong>Move from medical-only to lost-time</strong> within your first year or two.</li>
<li><strong>Add a complex jurisdiction</strong> like Texas or California to your resume.</li>
<li><strong>Earn designations</strong> such as the AIC or a workers compensation claims professional credential.</li>
<li><strong>Build litigation experience</strong> working with defense counsel and mediations.</li>
<li><strong>Consider a self-insured employer</strong> once you have five or more years of experience.</li>
</ol>

<p>To see how your current offer stacks up, try the <a href="/salary-calculator/">Adjuster Salary Calculator</a>. Workers comp adjuster salary may not have the spikes of storm work, but steady raises, benefits and remote options make it one of the most reliable careers in claims.</p>
